{
  "_comment": "AI loadout table: pydcs aircraft id -> role -> era -> {label, pylons}. The engine NEVER touches the player's loadout; this arms the AI only. pydcs cannot supply defaults server-side (it reads payload .lua files out of a DCS installation we do not have), so every AI aircraft ships CLEAN unless it appears here.",

  "_keys": "role is what the aircraft is FOR in this engine ('cap' = area patrol, 'bfm' = the merge), not a DCS task name. Era key is 'wwii' | 'coldwar' | 'modern' | '*' (any). Most airframes only ever appear in one era because TIER_CAP is already era-gated, so they carry a single '*' entry; airframes that CROSS eras (MiG-23MLD) carry explicit era keys and get period-correct stores in each. tests/test_loadouts.py resolves every (airframe, role, era) combination the threat pools can actually produce and fails on an illegal pylon, an unknown CLSID, or an anachronism.",

  "_pylons": "Keys are DCS station numbers as strings; values are CLSIDs. Every one is checked against that airframe's own PylonN legal-store list, which pydcs generates from the game's data — an entry that DCS would reject cannot reach a player.",

  "_label": "The human string is authored NEXT TO the pylons on purpose: the brief reads from the same record the engine loads, so the two can never drift.",

  "_light": "Optional 'light' sibling = the trimmed fit used at Threat Dial intensity 1-2. Era-typical means era-typical, not maximum: a MiG-29S with six missiles against a lone F-5 is a formality, not a fight.",

